WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.340
Frost and 32 of 36 Martian polar craters changes with local conditions rather than global seasons. This is Fascinating Frontiers Episode 240. After the polar craters, Ruben's hidden companions, Lucy among the Trojans, and Concordia's gel propellant, then a weekend review, an international team examined images from the high-resolution imaging science experiment, or high-rise camera on the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA, Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter,

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.080
here, according to Universe Today.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.640
The frames run from 2006 to 2025 and cover bright deposits in the northern polar region.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.060
Those deposits track seasonal frost growth and retreat inside the craters.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.260
Four of the craters showed no measurable change at all.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.700
The study concludes that local topography, crater geometry, and microclimatic conditions

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.580
control the frost cycles rather than planet-wide climate patterns.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:59.720
seasons. Seasonal frost on crater rims appears responsible for the observed brightness variations.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.380
Researchers noted that only a subset of craters show altered frost conditions relative to previous

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:12.548
years, while others maintain similar extents. The high rise record spans 12 Mars years and shows frost

00:01:12.548 --> 00:01:18.168
behavior differing sharply even between neighboring craters. Local slopes and rim heights apparently

00:01:18.168 --> 00:01:24.808
trap or redirect water vapor creating distinct micro environments inside each depression. Because these

00:01:24.808 --> 00:01:27.488
These pockets operate independently of the main polar cap.

00:01:27.988 --> 00:01:32.128
They may preserve records of past climate shifts that broader deposits have overwritten.

00:01:32.688 --> 00:01:37.168
Future orbiter campaigns targeting the same craters that identical seasonal phases could

00:01:37.168 --> 00:01:40.628
test whether the pattern repeats or drifts over longer time scales.

00:01:41.228 --> 00:01:45.868
Inter-annual frost changes remain localized instead of uniform across the polar cap.

00:01:46.819 --> 00:01:51.339
A crater only a few kilometers across on the Martian polar cap can hold several extra

00:01:51.339 --> 00:01:55.499
centimeters of frost each winter, while a nearby flat plane stays bare.

00:01:56.119 --> 00:02:00.359
The walls block direct sunlight and reduce wind speeds that would otherwise carry water

00:02:00.359 --> 00:02:03.479
vapor away, allowing ice to accumulate on the floor.

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:08.479
Over a single mars year, this extra layer grows thick enough to reflect sunlight and cool

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.799
the crater further, blocking in the cycle.

00:02:11.439 --> 00:02:16.079
Neighboring craters only a few kilometers apart can host frost deposits that differ by weeks

00:02:16.079 --> 00:02:18.839
in timing and by tens of percent in thickness.

00:02:18.839 --> 00:02:23.899
Those variations matter because frost is the visible tracer of water moving between

00:02:23.899 --> 00:02:28.819
the surface and the thin atmosphere. Each crater therefore acts as a miniature laboratory

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:33.999
recording how topography sorts and stores that water. What remains unknown is whether

00:02:33.999 --> 00:02:38.919
the frost that lingers longest inside certain craters also carries a distinct isotopic

00:02:38.919 --> 00:02:43.519
signature that could reveal how long the water has been cycling through the same sheltered

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:48.733
pocket. Preview data from the National Science Foundation and Department of Energy Vera

00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:54.833
C. Ruben Observatory, discussed on the space subreddit, revealed Aquarius 4, an ultra-faint

00:02:54.833 --> 00:03:02.213
galaxy 355,000 light years away. The same early frames turned up Ruben GC1, a star cluster

00:03:02.213 --> 00:03:07.593
on the far side of the Milky Way behind heavy dust. Both objects were identified in test

00:03:07.593 --> 00:03:13.093
images before the survey officially began. One study was led by a Yale astronomer using

00:03:13.093 --> 00:03:19.053
the early data from Sarah Pachone in Chile. Researchers expect the full 10-year survey to uncover

00:03:19.053 --> 00:03:24.733
hundreds more faint satellites. The discoveries provide new opportunities to test models of galaxy

00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:33.853
formation and trace the assembly history of the Milky Way. Dwarf Planet 2026ED 138 now sits 47.2

00:03:33.853 --> 00:03:40.333
astronomical units from the Sun and shines at magnitude 19. Archival plates show the object was

00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:46.413
recorded but not recognized in 1937. Its orbit solution is available through the Jet Propulsion

00:03:46.413 --> 00:03:52.373
Laboratory, or JPL, small-body database. The body was observed decades earlier yet remained

00:03:52.373 --> 00:03:57.893
unidentified until recent survey data confirmed its status. The current distance places it well

00:03:57.893 --> 00:04:03.914
beyond Neptune in the outer solar system. NASA's Lucy spacecraft has become the first probe to

00:04:03.914 --> 00:04:09.414
enter the Jupiter Trojan asteroid region, according to the Times of India. The journey has already

00:04:09.414 --> 00:04:12.334
covered more than 2.2 billion miles since launch.

00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:15.734
That distance was accumulated over five years of flight.

00:04:16.354 --> 00:04:20.774
The spacecraft reached the Trojan region after traveling farther than any previous mission

00:04:20.774 --> 00:04:22.014
to these ancient asteroids.

00:04:22.734 --> 00:04:25.634
The bodies are primitive objects that share Jupiter's orbit.

00:04:26.274 --> 00:04:29.954
Lucy's trajectory allows it to visit multiple Trojans over the coming years.

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:36.414
Sarah's holds 25-30% of the main asteroid belt's total mass, according to Universe Today.

00:04:36.414 --> 00:04:41.734
It is the largest object in the asteroid belt and the only dwarf planet inside Jupiter's

00:04:41.734 --> 00:04:47.774
orbit. Its surface shows bright salt deposits link to past cryovalcanic activity. The bright

00:04:47.774 --> 00:04:53.074
spots are highly reflective salt deposits that stand out against the darker regolith. Researchers

00:04:53.074 --> 00:04:58.774
are examining how water ice layers make and seal extensive salt accumulations. Those deposits

00:04:58.774 --> 00:05:04.218
are being read for what they show about the dwarf planet's internal evolution. Concordia

00:05:04.218 --> 00:05:09.598
university researchers developed a kerosene-based gel propellant that improves storage safety and

00:05:09.598 --> 00:05:15.958
flow control for rockets and satellites according to Fizz.org. The gel remains stable until ignited

00:05:15.958 --> 00:05:21.918
or pumped. The formulation reduces spill risks during ground handling. It allows precise control

00:05:21.918 --> 00:05:27.058
of thrust compared with traditional liquid fuels. Handling is easier than with those conventional

00:05:27.058 --> 00:05:32.418
liquids. The gel is being examined for both large launch vehicles and small satellite propulsion

00:05:32.418 --> 00:05:39.515
system's. SpaceX's fully stacked starship stands 407 feet tall, according to end-gaget.

00:05:39.835 --> 00:05:46.135
It generates 18.1 million pounds force of lift-off thrust from 33 Raptor engines on the super-heavy

00:05:46.135 --> 00:05:54.055
booster. The upper stage adds another 3.5 million pounds force. The booster alone measures 236.2 feet

00:05:54.055 --> 00:06:02.395
while the spacecraft section reaches 170.6 feet. Propelant capacity totals 5,250 metric tons across

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:07.935
both stages. Both stages are designed for recovery and reuse, and the vehicle is intended

00:06:07.935 --> 00:06:13.552
to carry large payloads and eventually support crude missions beyond Earth orbit. Radio

00:06:13.552 --> 00:06:18.832
images show Beetlejuice's atmosphere recovering from its recent dramatic dimming, while a companion

00:06:18.832 --> 00:06:24.312
object continues to plow through the star's outer layers, according to Space.com. The interaction

00:06:24.312 --> 00:06:29.692
disturbs the supergiant's extended atmosphere on observable time scales. The companion's

00:06:29.692 --> 00:06:34.252
Passage creates visible disturbances that astronomers can track across multiple wavelengths.

00:06:34.932 --> 00:06:39.352
Recovery from the dimming event is now visible in radio data that map the outer envelope.

00:06:40.359 --> 00:06:45.219
Chinese astronomers reported three exoplanets that have apparently endured extreme conditions

00:06:45.219 --> 00:06:48.519
around their host stars, according to Vietnam.vn.

00:06:49.119 --> 00:06:52.999
The system's challenge current models of planet survival in hostile environments.

00:06:53.639 --> 00:06:58.119
The planets appear to have maintained stable orbits despite intense stellar radiation and

00:06:58.119 --> 00:07:03.059
